assertThat(zipper.left.right.toTree()).isEqualTo(binaryTree);
```
`zipper.left.right` = node 3. `node3.toTree()` = `new BinaryTree(node3)`. And we compare to `binaryTree` (root = node 1). 

So `BinaryTree.equals` must be structural — it must reconstruct the full tree from the root zipper and compare. Since node 3's `up` pointer leads back to node 2 → node 1, `toTree()` needs to walk up to the actual root first!

Wait, that's the key insight. `toTree()` on a deep focus must return the whole tree, not just the subtree. Let me re-read.

`zipper.left.right.toTree()` — node 3. The expected result equals `binaryTree` which is the full tree (1 with children 2, 4). So `toTree()` must walk up to the root (following `up` pointers) and return a `BinaryTree` rooted at the top.

So `toTree()`:
```java
BinaryTree toTree() {
    Zipper z = this;
    while (z.up != null) z = z.up;
    return new BinaryTree(z);
}
```

Now `BinaryTree.equals` — compare structurally. Two BinaryTrees are equal if their trees are structurally equal (same values, same shape).

Let me define a helper to compare two zippers structurally:
```java
boolean same(Zipper a, Zipper b) {
    if (a == null && b == null) return true;
    if (a == null || b == null) return false;
    if (a.getValue() != b.getValue()) return false;
    return same(a.left, b.left) && same(a.right, b.right);
}
```

